content.content:
Skill "code-review" completed (forked execution).

Result:
Review of PR #2469 (`feature/seasonal-periods`, head `22ab6187`) complete. Scope note: the three-dot diff also carries #2447's schema commits, but `backend/app/db/models.py` and the migration are byte-identical to `main` for `seasonal_periods`, so the review covered only the real delta (service, scoring, API, authoring doors, executor, MCP docstrings). All nine findings were posted as inline comments on the PR (`--comment`).

```json
[
  {
    "file": "backend/app/services/seasonal_period_service.py",
    "line": 142,
    "summary": "The delete-in-use guard and attached_checks count are bypassed by any non-canonical UUID spelling in a check's config, because the JSONB containment filter only matches the canonical lower-case string while the authoring parser accepts upper case, braces, urn:uuid and 32-hex forms and check_service stores config as sent.",
    "failure_scenario": "create_check with config.seasonal_periods=[\"4C2A…\"] (upper case) passes require_seasonal_periods → admin DELETE /admin/seasonal-periods/{id} finds zero attached checks and succeeds → the next run logs seasonal_periods_missing and scores without the calendar; GET /{id} reports attached_checks 0 while the listing's attached_counts (which parses ids) counts 1."
  },
  {
    "file": "backend/app/services/dryrun_service.py",
    "line": 509,
    "summary": "The dry-run path takes a FOR SHARE lock on every attached calendar row and then holds it across the live warehouse measurement, although nothing is saved.",
    "failure_scenario": "A member previews an anomaly check against a slow Snowflake table (tens of seconds, or the dry-run timeout) → an admin DELETE of that calendar entry (FOR UPDATE) blocks for the whole duration; many concurrent previews pin the row indefinitely."
  },
  {
    "file": "backend/app/services/seasonal.py",
    "line": 373,
    "summary": "Any non-usable reason from _ratios (ZERO_LEVEL, NEGATIVE_VALUE), not only NO_EARLIER_YEAR, is treated as 'first year' inside a declared period, dropping the real reason and possibly skipping the run.",
    "failure_scenario": "Last year's sale occurrence exists in history but its own level was 0 → _ratios returns ZERO_LEVEL → result says first_year: true, and with value > level the run reads skipped / within_declared_period for a period that has an earlier occurrence."
  },
  {
    "file": "backend/app/services/seasonal_period_service.py",
    "line": 285,
    "summary": "PATCH kind change contradicts the SeasonalPeriodPatch docstring: the merge keeps the old kind's fields from the row, so sending only the new kind's fields is refused.",
    "failure_scenario": "PATCH {\"kind\":\"dated\",\"start_date\":…,\"end_date\":…} on a recurring entry → normalise sees start_month still set → 422 'start_month applies only to a recurring entry' unless the client also nulls all four recurring fields."
  },
  {
    "file": "backend/app/services/seasonal_period_service.py",
    "line": 234,
    "summary": "_series_spelling has no except_id, so on update the row matches its own old series spelling and a case-only series correction is silently reverted.",
    "failure_scenario": "The only entry of series 'summer sale' is PATCHed to series 'Summer Sale' → query returns its own old value → stored series stays 'summer sale'; response shows the change did nothing."
  },
  {
    "file": "backend/app/services/seasonal.py",
    "line": 319,
    "summary": "For weekly checks the matched period is snapped back to Monday, which can precede the earlier occurrence's start; only the upper bound is checked.",
    "failure_scenario": "period=week, last year's sale started on a Thursday → earlier.start + offset snaps to the Monday before the sale → the run is compared with a week mostly outside the occurrence while 'matched' reports it as the period's day. (Related: inside(day) tests the week's Monday only, so a Tue–Sun-starting period never applies to that week.)"
  },
  {
    "file": "backend/app/services/seasonal.py",
    "line": 310,
    "summary": "EARLIER_YEAR_IN_DECLARED_PERIOD is reported as the not-applied reason even when an ordinary candidate existed but merely had no stored point.",
    "failure_scenario": "1 year back falls in a sale, 2–3 years back are absent from history → _ratios finds no ratios and returns the promoted reason → the result says earlier years were in a declared period when the actual blocker is missing history (no_earlier_year)."
  },
  {
    "file": "backend/app/services/seasonal.py",
    "line": 281,
    "summary": "_ordinary() re-walks Calendar.inside for every history day on every priors() call, and score() computes _candidates twice.",
    "failure_scenario": "20 entries × 2 occurrences × ~1100 days × (1 + up-to-3 candidate level() calls) × 2 (the 'without' re-score in declared_payload) → thousands of containing() evaluations per check per run; a precomputed set of declared days in History.__init__ and passing the effect through would make it O(days)."
  },
  {
    "file": "backend/app/api/v1/admin_seasonal_periods.py",
    "line": 158,
    "summary": "User is imported only to be re-exported via __all__, a lint workaround no other admin router uses.",
    "failure_scenario": "Dead import kept alive by a misleading public export; drop both."
  }
]
```

Verified-benign along the way (recorded, not posted): `seasonal_periods` on a whole-table metric is refused at authoring because the key joined `_PERIOD_KEYS` and `_period_spec` rejects stray keys; `update_check`, `restore_check_version`, `import_suite` and `dry_run_check` all reach `require_seasonal_periods` (the only session-less caller is `llm_checksuggest`, which only shapes a suggestion); `declared_payload`'s `verdict_changed` uses the same `derive_status` on the same rounded `z_score` the executor writes to `metric_value`, so the with/without comparison is consistent; NUL screening comes from `ApiModel`'s before-validator.

